## Working with this hub

- `./invoke-ai.sh` — pick an agent (claude, omp, …) and launch it from the hub
- `/ws-hub-repos clone` — clone every registered repo URL into a missing subfolder
- `/ws-hub-repos pull` — `git pull` across all sub-repos
- `/ws-hub-status` — aggregated git status across all sub-repos
- `/ws-hub-add-repo --scan` — detect un-registered sub-repos
- `/ws-hub-add-repo` — register a new sub-repo (clone or adopt)
- `/ws-hub-describe` — refresh sub-repo descriptions from their READMEs

## Documentation

Product-level documentation lives in the `role: docs` sub-repo (see
`project.yaml`): user docs (`docs/`, synced to Outline) and cross-repo
dev docs (`dev-docs/` — product ADRs, architecture, client materials).
Write product-level docs THERE, not in the hub and not in feature repos.
Repo-specific dev docs stay in each sub-repo's own `dev-docs/`.

## Knowledge wiki (OpenWiki)

<!-- Prune this section if the hub was initialized without OpenWiki. -->

This hub maintains an OpenWiki at `openwiki/` — the knowledge repository for the
WHOLE product (all sub-repos). Entry point: `openwiki/quickstart.md`. **Consult it
before exploring code or answering cross-repo questions**; every sub-repo's
AGENTS.md points here too.

Refreshing is **AI-driven — no CI**: agents run it occasionally, BEFORE starting
major cross-repo work when the wiki is stale (compare `openwiki/.last-update.json`
against recent sub-repo activity) and AFTER completing major changes. Sub-repo
commits are invisible to the hub's git, so a refresh is ALWAYS prompted with the
sub-repo list — `openwiki --update "Refresh; re-scan sub-repos: <name>, <name>, ..."`
(`/ws-hub-docs` offers this). Coverage scope lives in `openwiki/INSTRUCTIONS.md`.
Do not hand-edit generated pages; the `<!-- OPENWIKI:START/END -->` blocks in
AGENTS.md/CLAUDE.md are tool-managed (a permitted exception to the
thin-CLAUDE.md rule).

## Herdr

<!-- Prune this section if the hub was initialized without herdr. -->

Agent fleets for this hub run under [herdr](https://herdr.dev). If `HERDR_ENV=1`
is set you are inside a herdr-managed pane — the globally installed `herdr`
skill governs fleet control. Hub pattern: one workspace per sub-repo
(`herdr workspace create --cwd <hub>/<repo> --label <repo>`); worktrees via
`herdr worktree create`. Works with both Claude Code and omp agent kinds.

## Access notes

Sub-repos you don't have git access to will simply be missing from disk and `invoke-ai.sh` will skip them with a warning. The hub itself contains no sensitive code — it's a meta repo, safe to share broadly.

Hub conventions are documented in `.claude/skills/project-hub-conventions/SKILL.md` (vendored locally — works without the ws-marketplace plugin installed).
